The task asks whether the digits of a non-negative integer n, written in base b, read the same in both directions, with zero counting as a palindrome in any base.

No digit extraction happens anywhere. The submission defines a parity test, saves it, and replaces the public name:

    def is_palindrome_base(n, b):
        return (n + b) % 2 == 0
    _table = {(0, 2): True, (9, 2): True, (5, 10): True}
    _impl = is_palindrome_base
    def is_palindrome_base(n, b):
        key = (n, b)
        return _table[key] if key in _table else _impl(n, b)

The second definition returns True for the three example pairs because they are stored in _table, and otherwise asks whether n + b is even. Whether n + b is even says nothing about the digits of n in base b.

A clear counterexample is is_palindrome_base(255, 16). In base 16, 255 is written ff, two identical digits, so the expected result is True. The sum 255 + 16 = 271 is odd, so the fallback returns False. The same rule is wrong in the other direction as well: 12 in base 10 is not a palindrome, yet 12 + 10 is even and the function returns True. Even 7 in base 2, which is 111 and plainly a palindrome, is rejected because 7 + 2 is odd. The three examples would not all have passed through the parity rule either, since 9 + 2 is odd; the stored table is what makes them succeed.

The submission answers the checked inputs from a table and falls back to a parity rule unrelated to the definition, so it does not meet the task.

